{"title":"2023 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 18","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_18\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 18</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_6_(Based_on_previous_knowledge)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6 (Based on previous knowledge)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_7_(Dual)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7 (Dual)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Solution_8_(Uses_Solution_2)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 8 (Uses Solution 2)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(Easy_To_Follow)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (Easy To Follow)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Little_Fermat\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Little Fermat</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Math-X_(First_fully_understand_the_problem!!!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Math-X (First fully understand the problem!!!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">14</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-15\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">15</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-16\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">16</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-17\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">17</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-18\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">18</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_18\">Problem 18</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 18\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A rhombic dodecahedron is a solid with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> congruent rhombus faces. At every vertex, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> edges meet, depending on the vertex. How many vertices have exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> edges meet?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/8/2580afd0a1812870ac5295fc7cac54f67e838373.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) }5\\qquad\\textbf{(B) }6\\qquad\\textbf{(C) }7\\qquad\\textbf{(D) }8\\qquad\\textbf{(E) }9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"355\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note Euler's formula where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad22c7606fe1d081de83ae284d6a68872ef79bf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{Vertices}+\\text{Faces}-\\text{Edges}=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"219\" height=\"17\" />. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> faces. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/9/e290f2cba0ba0e11c0b91630da15a408da2fcf1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> edges, because there are 12 faces each with four edges and each edge is shared by two faces. Now we know that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/a/5ba3ceb82c1c99f2741b4aa72a623b3668af3f14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2-12+24=14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"134\" height=\"15\" /> vertices. Now note that the sum of the degrees of all the points is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/f/5afbad0b221f0ef70186fe70b08e6fc1efdd8dfa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$48$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />(the number of edges times 2). Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff51845797a05fd68c53c81a189bc899dc2537b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=$\" width=\"29\" height=\"8\" /> the number of vertices with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> edges. Now we know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/2/29274c7c19e1309eae43282dc3cfc65c52b128c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3x+4(14-x)}{2}=24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"164\" height=\"40\" />. Solving this equation gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/c/f4c88176c5c945452bdfc22ba9c8b980c138c0c7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"93\" height=\"30\" />. ~aiden22gao ~zgahzlkw (LaTeX) ~ESAOPS (Simplified)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> be the number of vertices with 3 edges, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> be the number of vertices with 4 edges. Since there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/b/52bc68d39f6edfb9f56e0ced661c8a44fb8c04ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4*12}{2}=24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"93\" height=\"38\" /> edges on the polyhedron, we can see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/a/aba66e4b53a4e80910a36a2ed762a8eeb54de6bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3x+4y}{2}=24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"109\" height=\"38\" />. Then, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/a/49abb7703e97dc64758124cdc4afe5e76c72a342.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3x+4y=48$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"105\" height=\"17\" />. Notice that by testing the answer choices, (D) is the only one that yields an integer solution for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />. Thus, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/d/ced70e08fcbb7c9e3c5221a6a79e47dbc8637103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"56\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>~Mathkiddie\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>With <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> rhombi, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/8/728b89c9a630e145133c322e9017e206698e5352.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4\\cdot12=48$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"85\" height=\"14\" /> total boundaries. Each edge is used as a boundary twice, once for each face on either side. Thus we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/1/111396ba23a3079edec68594b57e3982301066b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{48}2=24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"66\" height=\"38\" /> total edges.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> be the number of vertices with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> edges (this is what the problem asks for) and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> be the number of vertices with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> edges. We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/8/33831f8485356ac29666b8192c675bd6c987c7d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3A + 4B = 48$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"113\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p>Euler's formula states that, for all convex polyhedra, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/e/01e200162741a1e1a98813eb54ef51ffc8f0eb40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V-E+F=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"120\" height=\"14\" />. In our case, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/8/43849f9f5fa73117bfc9976d7780bf100f7968a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V-24+12=2\\implies V=14.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"242\" height=\"16\" /> We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/3/b831312585cb4ab6697896f86b72accbddbc4b45.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A+B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" /> is the total number of vertices as we are given that all vertices are connected to either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> edges. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/b/14bf1d3ade4f57fda607dcaeac0af17a635ad45d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A+B=14.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"100\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>We now have a system of two equations. Solving the system yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/c/5ccd6c6ebb91961b39fc9476bce42f9de171b59b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"96\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>Even without Euler's formula, we can do a bit of answer guessing. From <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/9/fb9b9822f7356df7ef800e8db16a6e3e302deb08.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3A+4B=48$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"113\" height=\"16\" />, we take mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> on both sides.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/3/443609d9b9a0ef267833ca229cd83034d1b259f5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3A+4B\\equiv48\\pmod{4}\\]\" width=\"191\" height=\"18\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/4/a9415e6c8a23aaf879d4f213668a870a40a2a266.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3A\\equiv0\\pmod{4}\\]\" width=\"136\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/7/277808a033b0d645b6df93e4ec60050444c8ad88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"25\" height=\"14\" /> must be divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. We know that the factor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> will not affect the divisibility by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/7/277808a033b0d645b6df93e4ec60050444c8ad88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"25\" height=\"14\" />, so we remove the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Checking answer choices, the only one divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is indeed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/c/5ccd6c6ebb91961b39fc9476bce42f9de171b59b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A=\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"96\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~Technodoggo ~zgahzlkw (small edits) ~ESAOPS (LaTeX)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that Euler's formula is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/5/e551563c73fe9fe056a10d1fa69e465cee9c4b4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V+F-E=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"123\" height=\"16\" />. We know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/f/19f336e01cae7dff27258a68b87292bd01490511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F=12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"58\" height=\"14\" /> from the question. We also know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/a/33a1110b0d300bb6c374bc97a37b88a29c9fe756.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E = \\frac{12 \\cdot 4}{2} = 24$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"127\" height=\"38\" /> because every face has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> edges and every edge is shared by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> faces. We can solve for the vertices based on this information.\n</p><p>Using the formula we can find:<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/9/359bd551ab7af0ed1e627082a3096095aacaefab.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[V + 12 - 24 = 2\\]\" width=\"127\" height=\"14\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/8/59853268f01e5d7308f327b2520a3fc8c1c53cc5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[V = 14\\]\" width=\"56\" height=\"12\" />Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/d/e0d2bf360290fd61d1c1557e763f2622363b3d35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"13\" /> be the number of vertices with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> edges and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> be the number of vertices with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> edges. We know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/d/75d322c6bb76e8b603f362186852be122efb1d03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t+f = 14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"18\" /> from the question and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/3/2b3a44e3cf747cadf04563f6ba37ffff24332a37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3t + 4f = 48$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"102\" height=\"18\" />. The second equation is because the total number of points is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/f/5afbad0b221f0ef70186fe70b08e6fc1efdd8dfa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$48$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> because there are 12 rhombuses of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> vertices. Now, we just have to solve a system of equations.<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/5/fa501f43c787d0d85ed0ccd98b859ba3cf829740.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3t + 4f = 48\\]\" width=\"99\" height=\"16\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/9/839a15f160e018b19e0b88816794d60f8a4ad450.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3t + 3f = 42\\]\" width=\"99\" height=\"16\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/2/f62479e852798db3d9b6e63296785da1b4e28e50.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f = 6\\]\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/9/4b979f0a689c3fcbd2a68e62619276679dc1bcd2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[t = 8\\]\" width=\"39\" height=\"12\" />Our answer is simply just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/d/e0d2bf360290fd61d1c1557e763f2622363b3d35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"13\" />, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/d/ced70e08fcbb7c9e3c5221a6a79e47dbc8637103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"56\" height=\"28\" /> ~musicalpenguin\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Each of the twelve rhombi has two pairs of angles across from each other that must be congruent. If both pairs of angles occur at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-point intersections, we have a grid of squares. If both occur at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-point intersections, we would have a cube with six square faces. Therefore, two of the points must occur at a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-point intersection and two at a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-point intersection.\n</p><p>Since each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-point intersection has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> adjacent rhombuses, we know the number of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-point intersections must equal the number of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-point intersections per rhombus times the number of rhombuses over <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Since there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> rhombuses and two <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-point intersections per rhombus, this works out to be:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/f/22f4c4a8e3153865e9bbb2a5f6eefd2bf644d0e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2\\cdot12}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"46\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Hence: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/d/ced70e08fcbb7c9e3c5221a6a79e47dbc8637103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"56\" height=\"28\" /> ~hollph27 ~Minor edits by FutureSphinx\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6_.28Based_on_previous_knowledge.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6_(Based_on_previous_knowledge)\">Solution 6 (Based on previous knowledge)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6 (Based on previous knowledge)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that a rhombic dodecahedron is formed when a cube is turned inside out (as seen here), thus there are 6 4-vertices (corresponding to each face of the cube) and 8 3-vertices (corresponding to each corner of the cube). Thus the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/d/ced70e08fcbb7c9e3c5221a6a79e47dbc8637103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"56\" height=\"28\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_7_.28Dual.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7_(Dual)\">Solution 7 (Dual)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7 (Dual)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that a rhombic dodecahedron is the dual of a cuboctahedron. A cuboctahedron has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> triangular faces, which correspond to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/d/ced70e08fcbb7c9e3c5221a6a79e47dbc8637103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"56\" height=\"28\" /> vertices on a rhombic dodecahedron that have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> edges.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_8_.28Uses_Solution_2.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_8_(Uses_Solution_2)\">Solution 8 (Uses Solution 2)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 8 (Uses Solution 2)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Continue through solution 2 until you get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/a/49abb7703e97dc64758124cdc4afe5e76c72a342.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3x+4y=48$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"105\" height=\"17\" />. Then we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/e/d0e203aff36624d52c3d08dbfa14c15a898d9205.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3x = 48-4y = 4(12-y) = \\text{multiple of 4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"319\" height=\"20\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/1/cb15a16cca90d201094265ee529e9e2cfb113de8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> The only multiple of 4 answer choice is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/a/eaa8ab9bcb79f26ec5405b5d9cba1ff96d953f33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8 = \\boxed{(D)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"76\" height=\"32\" />\n</p><p>~KindToucan\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28Easy_To_Follow.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_(Easy_To_Follow)\">Video Solution (Easy To Follow)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution (Easy To Follow)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SFxfxXsJaN8&amp;t=7s\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SFxfxXsJaN8&amp;t=7s</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Little_Fermat\">Video Solution by Little Fermat</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Little Fermat\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/h2Pf2hvF1wE?si=KLoAqmdqx_C55pEq&amp;t=4063\">https://youtu.be/h2Pf2hvF1wE?si=KLoAqmdqx_C55pEq&amp;t=4063</a>\n~little-fermat\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_Math-X_.28First_fully_understand_the_problem.21.21.21.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Math-X_(First_fully_understand_the_problem!!!)\">Video Solution by Math-X (First fully understand the problem!!!)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Math-X (First fully understand the problem!!!)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/GP-DYudh5qU?si=fFif-OiVZnkdTTv0&amp;t=5105\">https://youtu.be/GP-DYudh5qU?si=fFif-OiVZnkdTTv0&amp;t=5105</a>\n</p><p>~Math-X\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/5OuzPFvJPEY\">https://youtu.be/5OuzPFvJPEY</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=14\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z-OCnHUwnj0\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z-OCnHUwnj0</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=15\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/0AG5XmWY-D8\">https://youtu.be/0AG5XmWY-D8</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=16\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zvKijDeiYUs\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zvKijDeiYUs</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_3\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=17\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/0ssjr8KjOzk\">https://youtu.be/0ssjr8KjOzk</a>\n</p><p>~Steven Chen (Professor Chen Education Palace, www.professorchenedu.com)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=18\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhombic_dodecahedron\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhombic_dodecahedron</a>\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><